00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:46.490
So let's start with an observation
that does come out of the research,

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:51.170
but also just out of my own experience,
and probably out of your experience.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:53.532
We are really bad at predicting.

00:01:56.664 --> 00:02:01.605
But we are really good at
retrospectively rationalizing.

00:02:01.605 --> 00:02:04.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:11.470
I mean that not to be cynical,
I am big on retrospective rationalization.

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:14.060
And I think it's an important
part of leadership,

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:18.870
because you do have to make sense
out of why people come to work.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.910
And in that sense leaders are always
looking back at things that have

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.610
happened and
explaining to people why it makes sense.

00:02:26.890 --> 00:02:31.310
But when you look at the track record,
when you combine our talent at

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:37.690
retrospective rationalization with
our incredible inability to predict.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:39.840
So I remember in the classroom.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.330
It was 1993 and I was doing a case study

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:50.420
of a software company
that had made a product

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:55.610
that could manipulate
photographic images digitally but

00:02:55.610 --> 00:03:00.900
on a low end personal
computer pretty efficiently.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:03.530
And the entrepreneur was from France.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:06.130
He and
his team had come over from Toulouse,

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:08.752
decided they'd be here in Silicon Valley.

00:03:08.752 --> 00:03:11.430
And they had set up,
his name was Bruno De Leon,

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.560
some of you may remember Bruno,
a real whiz kid in software.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:20.890
And had come up with tremendous
innovation that is still there In your

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:23.590
digital technology that you carry around.

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:28.820
And, we had the normal class session
where we do the case study and

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.300
the students are asking questions and
we're talking.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.350
And then at some point,
with his amazing French accent,

00:03:35.350 --> 00:03:37.790
I wish I could talk like that.

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:44.049
He starts to say, that,
now remember what year this is, 1993.

00:03:44.049 --> 00:03:46.740
The first browsers are just being created.

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:50.730
We're not talking about the World Wide Web
yet, there's some discussion of it.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:55.380
Of course, the Internet and BITNET and
other such networks already existed for

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:00.810
scientists and academics, but
the world's about to change.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:06.520
And he says, I envision a world
where in just a couple of years

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:11.520
everyone's carrying around small
computers with the main purpose

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.780
being to trade photographs back and
forth with each other.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:21.010
Okay, now the best part of this,
and I have it on film, okay,

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:25.130
is the look on my, so
I'm standing kind of off to the side.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:27.190
You know how they've got
the visitor there, and

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:28.640
he's sort of going on like this.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.230
And the students are looking at him, and

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:38.290
they're looking at me rolling their eyes,
okay.

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:43.320
Now maybe some of you are in that
class and so [LAUGH] at the end of

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:48.560
the term when the professor gets
graded by the students right?

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.780
So I'm looking through and well,
Barnett's okay on this and Barnett's.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:57.190
But get rid of this De Leon guy, and

00:04:57.190 --> 00:05:02.070
get a real business in here, because
this guy's a whack job, he's a crazy.

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.600
That's the problem.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:10.970
When we do predict the future,
nobody believes it, nobody believes it.

00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:15.430
Nobody believes it, and I remember sort of
giving lectures on industrial evolution.

00:05:15.430 --> 00:05:18.860
And a lot of the things we talked
about nowadays with disruption,

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:21.810
we were talking about it of
course even back in the 90s.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:28.723
And by about 96,
I remember teaching the strategy, 95.

00:05:28.723 --> 00:05:32.273
I was teaching the strategy
core course and

00:05:32.273 --> 00:05:37.440
one of the MBAs was sitting
right in the front center.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:38.280
She raises her hand.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:44.036
I'm going on about how industries change
and new firms come in and old firms die.

00:05:44.036 --> 00:05:48.070
And she says Professor Barnett.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:53.042
Now you have to realize that at that
time I had a lot more, well just,

00:05:53.042 --> 00:05:56.110
hair and red in my beard and everything.

00:05:56.110 --> 00:06:00.310
But nonetheless,
I was an ancient man to her and she said,

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:05.049
Professor Barnett, this sounds
like a theory of the olden days.

00:06:05.049 --> 00:06:10.791
[LAUGH] My gosh It's great,
I love teaching,

00:06:10.791 --> 00:06:15.090
I just love teaching here really.

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:19.140
When everyone in class feels like they
are the embodiment of human evolution up

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:19.755
to this moment.

00:06:19.755 --> 00:06:24.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] I mean it's kind of [LAUGH].

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:30.690
But that's okay, they're only outdone by,
yeah, when it comes to arrogance.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.200
So, it's [LAUGH], so we're all in the
same, [LAUGH] this is the pot calling the.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:41.165
So, yeah, I sort of looked at her and
said well, what do you mean?

00:06:41.165 --> 00:06:48.110
[LAUGH] And she says, well now,
everything's changed, everything's stable.

00:06:48.110 --> 00:06:50.172
We have Microsoft.

00:06:50.172 --> 00:06:55.430
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's easy to forget that we thought

00:06:55.430 --> 00:06:59.530
we had finally reached equilibrium, these
theories you've learned about equilibrium.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:02.980
Everything goes to equilibrium this and

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:06.190
equilibrium that, and equilibrium
price and equilibrium quantity.

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:09.340
And steady state technology and
sustainable advance.

00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:14.080
I don't know what all this stable
states are, I haven't seen them yet.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:15.660
I study growth and failure.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:16.630
I've been here since 91.

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:19.260
Talk about being at the right place and
the right time,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.130
there's no stability that I can find.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:26.670
But at the time, it seemed that way
because we had a giant firm that had

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:34.860
by then probably, I don't know, 90,
95% of the desktop organized for us.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:41.100
And things seemed to that bright, young
woman as now we're all really stable.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.750
And if I would have told her, well,
I'll tell you what I told her.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:50.389
I said, look, I don't know what's going to

00:07:50.389 --> 00:07:54.350
happen that's going to make
us forget about Microsoft.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:57.948
But whatever it is,
it's going to be bodacious.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.890
And the whole class looks at me like,
that's all you got?

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:09.960
You get this bright question and
all you can use is an adjective?

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:15.920
The trouble is back to
predicting the future.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.780
If I could have gone forward, I had
gotten in that car that takes you to and

00:08:19.780 --> 00:08:21.780
looked at the world then gone back and
said.

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:26.470
You're not going to believe it,
it's going to be this college dorm project

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:30.300
where that guy in 93,
he appears to have been right.

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:38.710
But really just trading pictures of my
grandson back and forth with everyone.

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:42.453
Even if we could predict the future,
we wouldn't believe it.

00:08:45.130 --> 00:08:48.850
This is a problem,
if you're a business school professor.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:55.990
Seriously, because if you think about it,
if I'm in your shoes,

00:08:55.990 --> 00:09:02.130
as an alumnus or
if you think of the current students.

00:09:02.130 --> 00:09:05.030
They want to go out into the world and
be great and

00:09:05.030 --> 00:09:07.120
they want to understand that process.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:10.060
And part of becoming
great is coming here and

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:12.830
meeting each other and
becoming part of that network.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:17.710
And there's just that incredible life and
everything else that goes with it.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:22.580
But from a business school professor's
perspective I want us to be able to

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:27.250
give you some tools that will help you
increase your chances of being great.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:32.870
But this is a world where we have proven
ourselves to be very bad at predicting

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:39.900
which is a quandary if you're me and
my colleagues like me.

00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:44.590
Well, of course we've known about
this quandary for some time and

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:49.840
we could all tell stories of companies
that discover their greatness.

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:55.678
You may remember Apple computer

00:09:55.678 --> 00:10:00.150
when it was a computer company, if we

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:06.090
just look at the 2000 to 2016 period.

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:08.540
Nevermind everything that went on before,
a lot of folks in here,

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.450
we can talk about that,
that was also fascinating.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:17.220
But if you go just back to the year 2000,
Steve Jobs was being rehabilitated.

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:24.630
He had just came back to Apple,
had been fired several years before.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.770
On the East Coast,
there's a business school, which in 1996,

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:31.255
featured Steve Jobs as
the example of how not to lead.

00:10:31.255 --> 00:10:34.890
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It was their first case study,

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:36.050
the Donna Dubinsky case.

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:40.201
He fired Donna because she thought and

00:10:40.201 --> 00:10:44.620
talked at the same time, she leaned in.

00:10:44.620 --> 00:10:47.679
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:10:47.679 --> 00:10:52.680
the downside of leading in.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:56.400
And of course she's amazing as a leader,

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:00.980
went on to be a very successful
entrepreneur, now venture capitalist.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:04.610
And so, that particular
leadership development program,

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:05.790
which is a very good program.

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:12.720
Led off with the Donna Dubinsky case,
depicting, that's not how to be a leader.

00:11:12.720 --> 00:11:14.770
I can tell you that by about 2004,

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:18.130
when I would give lectures like
this anywhere in the world,

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:22.730
I often ask the question, who's a great
leader, to sort of warm up the crowd.

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:25.780
And depending on where I am,
you get really interesting answers,

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:32.401
see in Zagreb,
the answer was Genghis Khan.

00:11:32.401 --> 00:11:37.270
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay, in Peoria, it was Jesus Christ.

00:11:38.690 --> 00:11:42.040
The prophet Mohammed was raised
as a possible candidate for

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.120
a great leader in Dubai.

00:11:45.550 --> 00:11:49.106
I could go down the list
in Moscow you can guess.

00:11:49.106 --> 00:11:52.597
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So

00:11:52.597 --> 00:11:57.535
that was Putin but that was close.

00:11:57.535 --> 00:12:04.430
[LAUGH] So, but they always say
Steve Jobs, they always say Steve Jobs.

00:12:04.430 --> 00:12:10.010
So you go from being how not to
lead in 95, 96, to being ranked,

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:13.380
eight years later, alongside Jesus Christ,
the prophet Mohammed.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:17.200
And Genghis Khan, as one of the great
leaders to have ever existed in human

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:20.095
history, we are so bad at predicting.

00:12:20.095 --> 00:12:21.760
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We are so

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.520
good at retrospectively rationalizing.

00:12:24.520 --> 00:12:27.556
And how easily we forget, and
I think you can relate with me on this,

00:12:27.556 --> 00:12:29.491
because you remember some of these times.

00:12:29.491 --> 00:12:37.500
So iTunes introduced in 2001,
with no iPad, and no store.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.780
It was a jukebox,

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:42.310
where the strategy was to make
the desktop computer more valuable.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:46.400
So that maybe Apple could
pull out of its 3% of

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:51.620
market share doldrums versus
the Windows-based Intel Windows PCs.

00:12:51.620 --> 00:12:53.140
That was actually
the strategy at the time.

00:12:54.930 --> 00:13:00.460
Couple of years later,
the store is introduced, and

00:13:00.460 --> 00:13:05.230
Apple is shocked by the 10
million downloads of songs.

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:08.610
They suddenly find out they're selling
more music than they are computers

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:10.740
in 2003 and the strategy begins to change.

00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:17.360
Do you remember the hell
freezes over announcement?

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:24.830
Remember the strategy was to have
the Macintosh beat the PC, on

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:28.890
the desktop but suddenly they're selling
more music than they are computers.

00:13:29.890 --> 00:13:32.478
And that's just among
people with a Macintosh,

00:13:32.478 --> 00:13:36.024
because iTunes only functioned at
that time on an Apple computer.

00:13:38.125 --> 00:13:43.919
So Steve Jobs stepped out and
put up a big, the black outfit, and

00:13:43.919 --> 00:13:49.620
the big screen with the, and
it said hell freezes over.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:50.812
Anybody remember what they did?

00:13:57.359 --> 00:14:01.640
They released iTunes on Windows, that's
right, they released iTunes on Windows.

00:14:03.230 --> 00:14:08.560
And immediately, within two months,
there were another 15 million downloads,

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:11.450
and they completely shifted the strategy.

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:13.530
Let's not worry about, in fact,
within a couple of years,

00:14:13.530 --> 00:14:17.790
they would drop the name computer from
their name, they become a music company.

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:23.910
It's often the case that we discover
greatness, what's interesting,

00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:28.600
if they had stayed to plan,
that wouldn't have happened.

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:31.990
If they had stayed to plan,
that wouldn't have happened, of course,

00:14:31.990 --> 00:14:32.850
we all have plans.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:37.580
But the interesting thing on the
leadership side was the ability of their

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:40.700
leadership not to predict the future but

00:14:40.700 --> 00:14:43.990
to create a system that
discovers that future.

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.700
And of course I raise Apple
as an example of that but

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:52.150
there are plenty of other examples,
I would guess that right now.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:55.980
If we went around this room,
we would have dozens and dozens and

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:59.630
dozens of examples of your own companies,
or

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:04.300
companies you've been with in your
careers, that have discovered greatness.

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:10.140
Because in the years, since

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:15.200
about 1985 plenty of research has been
done to trace the histories of firms and

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:19.830
it looks like it is not the exception
to see the likes of an Apple.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:22.690
Of course Apple was
possibly the most valuable

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:25.670
company in the world right
now is quite exceptional but

00:15:25.670 --> 00:15:30.030
that story of discovering greatness
is the rule, not the exception.

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:35.650
Let's talk about Semex we're
in the theater that is

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:40.835
endowed by Semex and so
we're required to talk about Semex.

00:15:40.835 --> 00:15:43.237
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:15:43.237 --> 00:15:45.510
&gt;&gt; Mr Zambrano, so no, seriously though.

00:15:45.510 --> 00:15:48.070
So I love the Cemex
case because everybody,

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.710
when they hear these discovery stories,
Airbnb.

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:55.680
It starts out with three techies and
they lose a roommate and

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:59.250
if you're a techie whenever you have
a need, you write a webpage, right?

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:02.010
So if you're hungry, you write a webpage,
you're lonely, you write a webpage.

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:05.880
They lost a roommate,
let's write a webpage.

00:16:07.020 --> 00:16:08.720
Sure enough, people start landing on it.

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:12.660
They want to rent the couch and gosh,
we can make this a business and then they

00:16:12.660 --> 00:16:19.040
want to make it so that events like South
by Southwest or they were politicos.

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:23.080
So around 2007, 2008 at
the Democratic National Convention,

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:25.790
the first of the Obama Conventions,
they said

00:16:25.790 --> 00:16:29.930
we'll use this website to help people find
a place when they go to the convention.

00:16:31.300 --> 00:16:34.100
It really didn't work very well for
event organizing.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:38.795
It turns out people plan when they go
to conventions and they get a room.

00:16:38.795 --> 00:16:42.150
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They don't just show up in town,

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:43.125
my god, a room.

00:16:43.125 --> 00:16:44.239
Right?

00:16:44.239 --> 00:16:46.947
[LAUGH] They'll make a webpage.

00:16:46.947 --> 00:16:53.030
[LAUGH]
And one of the big problems they

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:56.520
had making it work were all these people
were trying to use the website just to

00:16:56.520 --> 00:17:00.265
rent from each other and they were trying
to figure out how could we stop this?

00:17:00.265 --> 00:17:03.230
[LAUGH] Don't they
understand our strategy?

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:04.150
We're trying to do event.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:09.140
If they had succeeded in their plan,
they would have been worth millions.

00:17:10.145 --> 00:17:13.710
[LAUGH]
Okay,

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:16.960
that reaction tells me you have some
sense of what Airbnb is worth now.

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:18.340
I don't know, is it $30 billion?

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:21.570
I don't know what the number is now,
more than Marriott.

00:17:21.570 --> 00:17:23.465
At some point, they said, wait a minute,

00:17:23.465 --> 00:17:28.610
this bothersome problem we have of all
these people trying to use the website.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:33.485
[LAUGH] We're trying to
improve the desktop.

00:17:33.485 --> 00:17:36.125
Wait a minute,
we're selling how much music?

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:40.760
This discovery process goes
on in company after company.

00:17:40.760 --> 00:17:42.930
And a lot of people think
it's only tech companies.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.700
It happened at CEMEX.

00:17:44.700 --> 00:17:49.199
And some of you will remember this because
we have an age old Cemex case I still keep

00:17:49.199 --> 00:17:51.260
in the curriculum because I love it.

00:17:51.260 --> 00:17:54.363
People think I'm required to teach it but
I'm not, I love it.

00:17:54.363 --> 00:18:01.010
Mr. Zambrano Sr.,

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:06.950
decades ago, had acquired
a couple of companies in Spain.

00:18:06.950 --> 00:18:11.982
The intent, the strategy was to smooth
out the business cycle for CEMEX.

00:18:11.982 --> 00:18:17.010
Maybe because cement is
very based on construction,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:20.160
very cyclical, high fixed cost business.

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:25.210
You're operating only in Mexico, then
you'll really be holding to that business

00:18:25.210 --> 00:18:30.170
cycle and he thought,
if we were in multiple countries,

00:18:30.170 --> 00:18:32.580
we would have counter cyclical
movement of the business cycle.

00:18:33.770 --> 00:18:37.760
And that was especially difficult at
the time because now we're a little bit

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.390
better with financial hedging for
dealing with that kind of cyclicality.

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:46.950
But finance markets had a little more
trouble with that at the time CEMEX as

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.530
one of the Mexican grupos at the time had

00:18:49.530 --> 00:18:52.979
also more trouble accessing some of
those international finance markets.

00:18:54.425 --> 00:18:59.350
They get over to Spain to do
the typical post merger integration,

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:06.110
they send over their team of managers and
engineers trained at Monterey Tech.

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:14.610
These folks are wiz kids when it comes
to state of the art manufacturing,

00:19:14.610 --> 00:19:17.920
operations, logistics, supply chains,

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:22.198
they put in truck tracking,
they clean up the accounting systems.

00:19:22.198 --> 00:19:26.210
They dramatically improve the performance
of the Spanish facilities.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:30.380
It turns out, most cement companies
in the world, decades ago,

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:35.950
were sleepy monopolies because of the
nature of cement and transportation costs,

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:40.890
they're typically the only game in town,
often government sponsored and so forth.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:45.280
CEMEX was vastly more efficient
because of the fact that they hire so

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:48.144
much out of Monterey Tech.

00:19:48.144 --> 00:19:54.470
And at that point,
Zambrano says wait a minute, if we're that

00:19:54.470 --> 00:20:00.560
good that we can dramatically improve the
performance of those Spanish acquisitions,

00:20:00.560 --> 00:20:05.545
let's write up a set of routines on that,
and now lets do it to ourselves.

00:20:05.545 --> 00:20:09.010
It's one of the few firms I
know that have the courage

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:12.450
to post-merger integrate themselves.

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:15.970
because we all like to post-merger
integrate other people, and

00:20:15.970 --> 00:20:18.770
some of you have done that, or
been on the receiving end of it.

00:20:18.770 --> 00:20:21.520
But this company said, look,
if we can do it to them,

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.790
we've got plants and
facilities we acquired around Mexico, so

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:27.140
they dramatically improved
their own performance.

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:28.050
Then as many of you know,

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:33.420
went on a very long run of
acquisitions where they would go in,

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:38.490
do post-merger integration, dramatically
improve the efficiency of operations.

00:20:38.490 --> 00:20:41.580
The world's finance markets
fall in love with them.

00:20:41.580 --> 00:20:45.080
What becomes their strategy
was discovered and

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:48.650
that's the cement industry,
so it's not only in tech.

00:20:48.650 --> 00:20:52.000
You see the discovery of why you're
great even in the cement industry.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:56.350
You shop at Trader Joe's,
you know when they started?

00:20:56.350 --> 00:20:59.110
All you people don't shop at Trader Joe's?

00:21:00.940 --> 00:21:03.770
because I know there's more
wine drinkers out there than,

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:06.420
you're smiling if nothing else,
you must be.

00:21:08.800 --> 00:21:12.886
They started out selling cigarettes and
ammunition.

00:21:12.886 --> 00:21:16.053
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Seriously, I knew Joe Coulombe,

00:21:16.053 --> 00:21:22.340
I had case studies on it, it's how they
started, just went through an evolution.

00:21:22.340 --> 00:21:25.730
Now they're selling boutique honey and
names of cheese I can't pronounce.

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:31.935
By the way, don't go there now looking for
cigarettes and ammunition.

00:21:31.935 --> 00:21:37.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You'd be arrested for the cigarettes.

00:21:37.410 --> 00:21:38.558
The ammunition would be okay.

00:21:38.558 --> 00:21:40.825
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's America.

00:21:40.825 --> 00:21:46.415
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Sorry, I digress.

00:21:46.415 --> 00:21:52.180
[LAUGH] So it's interesting, plenty of
non technology firms go through it too.

00:21:52.180 --> 00:21:56.020
Now here's the point though that makes
business school professors happy

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:56.530
about this.

00:21:58.230 --> 00:22:03.568
It isn't just dumb luck If you
tell enough of these stories,

00:22:03.568 --> 00:22:06.610
then people start thinking, what
Barnett's getting at is it's a crapshoot.

00:22:07.663 --> 00:22:12.500
It turns out some people tend to get
consistently more lucky than others.

00:22:14.945 --> 00:22:19.618
At some point, it stops being luck, when
some people are having all the luck, and

00:22:19.618 --> 00:22:20.920
some people aren't.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:25.320
When some companies are repeatedly
going through a discovery process, and

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.520
some are not.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.220
And that's because the discovery process,

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:35.220
while it is not good at predicting
the outcome, does follow a pattern.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:41.150
It always follows the same pattern,
regardless of where you see it happen,

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:44.400
three things always happen
in the discovery process.

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:50.841
If we understand these three things that
happened in the discovery process, we as

00:22:50.841 --> 00:22:55.956
leaders can make it more likely that our
own company get lucky more than it should.

00:22:58.369 --> 00:23:00.460
I want to talk about those three things.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.840
It's fun to tell stories
about companies that

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:07.600
discovered something much greater
than they had planned to do.

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:11.420
But it's a lot more fun to drill down and

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:15.740
think about what are those three
underlying processes that always seem to

00:23:15.740 --> 00:23:20.178
go on when companies
are good at discovery.

00:23:20.178 --> 00:23:21.730
I want to talk about that, but

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:25.608
if people have questions,
sometimes this is a nice time.

00:23:25.608 --> 00:23:28.950
We're kind of into the storytelling,
and now you're all sitting and

00:23:28.950 --> 00:23:32.180
you've had your coffee, so
if there's questions, you can.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:33.579
That's fine, I'll just keep talking.

00:23:36.224 --> 00:23:39.081
You want to know the three things.

00:23:39.081 --> 00:23:40.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:43.552
they're in a book that I wrote.

00:23:43.552 --> 00:23:46.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] No,

00:23:46.580 --> 00:23:47.670
actually I did write a book.

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:49.230
But you should not buy my book.

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:50.700
I gotta tell you, do not buy my book.

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:55.200
My wife, God love, Yvette and
I have been together for 35 years, and so

00:23:56.960 --> 00:24:02.245
She read my book and described it as
