00:25:43.090 --> 00:25:47.120
people correct me and that's, so
I'm being a little selfish with that, but

00:25:47.120 --> 00:25:50.760
at least the ideas are free there so
I put my ideas there.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:53.220
But I will tell you,
let's talk about those three things, okay?

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:54.539
Let's talk about those three things.

00:26:01.885 --> 00:26:06.369
And the first thing we need to talk
about comes down to what happens

00:26:06.369 --> 00:26:10.330
when you have a good idea,
an innovative idea.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:14.140
Now, when you have an innovative idea

00:26:16.990 --> 00:26:20.040
does everybody have to agree with
your idea for it to be a good one?

00:26:21.500 --> 00:26:22.530
No, of course not.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:26.610
In fact, it's obviously a rhetorical
question, right, you can see why.

00:26:26.610 --> 00:26:31.450
If nothing else, you wouldn't come
to a Stanford event and expect, no,

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:36.366
I only want to think about something
if everyone, It's kind of being here,

00:26:36.366 --> 00:26:43.686
it's kind of When you have an innovative
idea, what's the first thing you do?

00:26:46.500 --> 00:26:47.770
You tell somebody, exactly.

00:26:47.770 --> 00:26:48.380
You tell somebody.

00:26:49.480 --> 00:26:50.780
What if they don't like it,
what do you do?

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:53.310
You tell somebody else, absolutely.

00:26:53.310 --> 00:26:55.840
There's plenty of research on this,
that's exactly what happens.

00:26:55.840 --> 00:26:57.746
Hopefully, you tell somebody else,
hopefully,

00:26:57.746 --> 00:26:59.886
who hasn't talked to the first
person you told because.

00:27:03.702 --> 00:27:08.211
It's interesting though,
because less than 60 seconds ago you

00:27:08.211 --> 00:27:12.650
told me it really didn't matter,
but it appears to matter a lot.

00:27:17.860 --> 00:27:21.110
If we can have a good idea regardless of
whether everybody's agreeing with us why

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:26.990
are we trying to find people who
agree with us, what's going on?

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:32.360
What is it about having, why are we
feeling compelled to ask other people

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:37.110
if we think our innovative idea could be
right, even if they don't agree with us?

00:27:37.110 --> 00:27:37.800
What's going on?

00:27:39.420 --> 00:27:40.890
Okay, so we're looking for validation.

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:45.630
&gt;&gt; We want to execute the plan.

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:47.550
&gt;&gt; We want to be able to execute it,
right?

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:49.420
So we might want validation, but

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:52.080
the other side is we might
want to make sure we could do.

00:27:52.080 --> 00:27:55.675
So if they disagree it's not so
much that we think we're wrong,

00:27:55.675 --> 00:27:59.775
we just realized we got a bit of
a wall to get past to get it, right?

00:27:59.775 --> 00:28:02.425
We've got to line our ducks up,
which is an interesting.

00:28:03.645 --> 00:28:06.004
In fact,
let's play with this idea a little bit.

00:28:09.059 --> 00:28:10.910
Let's play with this idea a little bit.

00:28:10.910 --> 00:28:13.026
When you have an innovative idea,

00:28:19.159 --> 00:28:20.933
You might be right, can you see that?

00:28:23.466 --> 00:28:25.644
Okay.

00:28:25.644 --> 00:28:26.859
But you might be wrong.

00:28:31.219 --> 00:28:32.280
You might be wrong.

00:28:33.380 --> 00:28:35.470
This isn't like good, bad.

00:28:35.470 --> 00:28:37.290
It's like correct, incorrect.

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:40.070
Okay.
[LAUGH] You might be right but

00:28:40.070 --> 00:28:41.510
you might be wrong and you know it.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.460
If you think about it when
it comes to innovation,

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:48.420
if you know you're right,
it's probably not an innovative idea.

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:51.940
Right if you gotta kind of.

00:28:59.394 --> 00:29:03.844
So when you ask other people and they're
agreeing with you you'd like to know

00:29:03.844 --> 00:29:07.884
whether it's giving you a clue about
whether you're right or wrong,

00:29:07.884 --> 00:29:11.600
the trouble is you don't know,
maybe they don't know either.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:17.223
And so you know in your heart when
you ask other people that if they're

00:29:17.223 --> 00:29:22.195
agreeing with you all it's really telling
you is that the idea is consensus.

00:29:24.983 --> 00:29:26.291
That's all it's really telling you.

00:29:28.463 --> 00:29:33.292
And if you're getting pushback
all it's really telling

00:29:33.292 --> 00:29:36.820
you is that it's a non-consensus idea.

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:43.520
Whether it's right or wrong, one thing for
sure I'm getting a lot of pushback.

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:45.310
This is a non-consensus idea.

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:51.630
And the problem with discovering greatness

00:29:53.070 --> 00:29:57.550
is that all four of those are possible and
we know it.

00:29:59.560 --> 00:30:01.580
We know that every time we're
thinking of something innovative,

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:03.380
we know that that's the case.

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:06.560
So let's work through those possibilities.

00:30:07.640 --> 00:30:10.170
And the best way to structure it
is to start with the assumption

00:30:10.170 --> 00:30:10.820
that you're right.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:14.190
Now you can't know you're right and
have it be innovative.

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:18.750
But let's assume somehow you
did know that you are right.

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:22.126
So if you knew you were right,
where would you rather be?

00:30:27.532 --> 00:30:28.510
What's the argument?

00:30:28.510 --> 00:30:31.100
So I've heard both coming up.

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:33.489
So what's the argument for consensus?

00:30:40.421 --> 00:30:43.810
Okay, everybody agrees with you so
we're back to the execution point.

00:30:43.810 --> 00:30:46.470
We're definitely going to
be able to execute this.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:51.450
What's the argument for non-consensus?

00:30:51.450 --> 00:30:53.201
Okay we can make it better
through the dialectic.

00:31:00.657 --> 00:31:01.270
Okay.

00:31:01.270 --> 00:31:01.770
Okay, yeah.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:05.290
&gt;&gt; You'll certainly be out in front.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:08.550
Right.
You're going to be out in front.

00:31:08.550 --> 00:31:11.960
But it's going to be hard to execute,
we gotta give the execution people this.

00:31:11.960 --> 00:31:13.120
Now, you're the execution guy.

00:31:13.120 --> 00:31:17.190
For the rest of the day you're like Mr.
Execution, which can be good.

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:22.590
So this is the way I think of it,
this is going to be hard to execute,

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:26.700
but if you do execute,
you can tell I'm not a finance professor,

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:30.456
there would be Greek letter here,
instead the symbol I'm going to use is.

00:31:30.456 --> 00:31:33.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; A strategy professor,

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:38.590
this is a great place to be,
right, but this is Irwin Jacobs.

00:31:38.590 --> 00:31:43.140
Irwin Jacobs and
his fellow scientist Viterbi

00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:48.500
started Qualcomm based on CDMA technology,
a wireless technology that was

00:31:48.500 --> 00:31:52.900
very controversial and
a very non-consensus idea.

00:31:52.900 --> 00:31:57.230
DARPA had given up on CDMA,
they thought that It was so complicated,

00:31:57.230 --> 00:32:01.310
they created it with,
sponsored the research that created

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:06.550
Code Division Multiple Access Technology,
and they wanted it to

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:11.600
help encode messages that the Soviet Union
could then not intercept.

00:32:11.600 --> 00:32:14.550
So fun to be able to talk to a crowd who
understands what the Soviet Union is,

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:16.510
because I normally then have to explain-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:18.276
&gt;&gt; That there was this empire,

00:32:18.276 --> 00:32:23.132
it was a giant empire, it's true,
it actually existed, and then [LAUGH], but

00:32:23.132 --> 00:32:28.080
it's very, and then they'll say,
you're talking about Russia, well now.

00:32:29.120 --> 00:32:32.670
So, they so gave up, and was so

00:32:32.670 --> 00:32:34.390
complicated they put it
out in a public domain.

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:36.713
And they said, even the Russians
can't figure this out, I mean,

00:32:36.713 --> 00:32:37.455
it's just you know.

00:32:37.455 --> 00:32:42.000
We'll just put it out there,
and so very non-consensus,

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:46.190
banned in Europe because of it,
banned in Europe.

00:32:46.190 --> 00:32:50.260
Because they wanted TDMA only for GSM.

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:53.100
Create a real problem when
the smartphone comes along,

00:32:53.100 --> 00:32:55.030
since CDMA was a lot
better at carrying data.

00:32:56.960 --> 00:32:58.230
And we're so bad at predicting.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:01.655
But we're very good at
retrospectively rationalizing it.

00:33:01.655 --> 00:33:06.470
So,they own a lot of IP at Qualcomm,
and that leads to this outcome.

00:33:06.470 --> 00:33:07.720
Because when you're the only game,

00:33:07.720 --> 00:33:11.900
when you're the only one who thinks you're
right, then you might end up owning

00:33:11.900 --> 00:33:14.900
a lot of the IP, because nobody else
is even going to do research on it.

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:18.580
Now here you can execute, the trouble
is everybody is executing on it.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:24.030
Right, which from a strategic perspective,
it's not a bad thing,

00:33:24.030 --> 00:33:25.506
but we're all doing it.

00:33:25.506 --> 00:33:29.458
So this you can execute,
this is going to be hard to execute, but

00:33:29.458 --> 00:33:31.680
if you can, the payoff is now.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:33.787
What if you're wrong,
where do you want to be if you're wrong?

00:33:35.261 --> 00:33:37.838
Absolutely, consensus, no big story,

00:33:37.838 --> 00:33:42.116
[LAUGH] it's just humans, when
they're wrong do not want to be alone.

00:33:42.116 --> 00:33:44.380
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:45.540
It's as simple as that.

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:51.390
It's just as simple as that,
I mean, [LAUGH] your kid asks you.

00:33:53.300 --> 00:33:56.680
My wife and I have six kids, so I've had
this questions come up a number of times,

00:33:56.680 --> 00:33:59.360
or the,
the statement come up a number of times.

00:33:59.360 --> 00:34:03.210
Your kid comes home and says, Dad,
this is the latest rendering of it.

00:34:04.750 --> 00:34:07.290
Everybody at school-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:07.290 --> 00:34:08.502
&gt;&gt; Yeah, never a good start to any

00:34:08.502 --> 00:34:09.160
conversation.

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:11.014
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:11.014 --> 00:34:12.954
&gt;&gt; Everybody at school had a lot of

00:34:12.954 --> 00:34:14.826
trouble on the history test.

00:34:14.826 --> 00:34:18.114
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:34:18.114 --> 00:34:20.206
&gt;&gt; Is your daughter suddenly concerned

00:34:20.206 --> 00:34:22.637
about the state of education in Palo Alto?

00:34:22.637 --> 00:34:25.912
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's kind of [LAUGH].

00:34:25.912 --> 00:34:27.402
You know she's calibrating you.

00:34:27.402 --> 00:34:30.306
Right, she's trying to get you over here,

00:34:30.306 --> 00:34:34.870
because then we can talk about,
you know, and then it's not so bad.

00:34:34.870 --> 00:34:39.457
Okay I lost money in 2000, but
we all lost money in 2000.

00:34:39.457 --> 00:34:40.920
So for me it was Webvan.

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:44.146
I don't know what yours was, but you know.

00:34:44.146 --> 00:34:45.881
This guy.

00:34:45.881 --> 00:34:47.805
He's a fool.

00:34:47.805 --> 00:34:48.800
This guy is a fool.

00:34:50.340 --> 00:34:51.540
We told him he was wrong.

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:53.520
He did it anyway.

00:34:55.220 --> 00:34:55.760
And he's wrong.

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:03.020
And the problem with
discovering great business.

00:35:03.020 --> 00:35:08.330
And the number one problem
with the discovery process,

00:35:08.330 --> 00:35:15.390
is that humans fear being a fool,
much more than they hope to be a genius.

00:35:18.420 --> 00:35:21.920
And a result, they're quiet
about their non-consensus ideas.

00:35:21.920 --> 00:35:26.631
They're asking people,
they realize it's non- consensus.

00:35:26.631 --> 00:35:32.376
And in fact, you should ask yourself this,
your people in your organization,

00:35:32.376 --> 00:35:38.568
are they quiet about their non-consensus
ideas, for fear they might be seen a fool?

00:35:40.486 --> 00:35:42.836
And if so,
what does that say about your leadership?

00:35:46.401 --> 00:35:52.880
Because some places are safer than others,
when it comes to non-consensus behavior.

00:35:52.880 --> 00:35:58.080
Some people, this is a career ender,
some places, this is a career ender.

00:35:59.920 --> 00:36:02.802
And if this is a career ender,
you're never going to hear that.

00:36:07.164 --> 00:36:11.740
This brings up the first property of
all systems that discover greatness.

00:36:12.860 --> 00:36:15.960
And that is, they are systems
that generate variability.

00:36:17.680 --> 00:36:19.040
They generate variability.

00:36:21.030 --> 00:36:23.760
Now notice I didn't say
they generate greatness.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:27.556
I said they generate variability, and
let me explain why I put it that way.

00:36:30.671 --> 00:36:32.443
You can tell I'm an old chalkboard guy.

00:36:48.187 --> 00:36:49.970
If you think about the innovative process.

00:36:51.190 --> 00:36:54.460
All innovative processes,
including companies,

00:36:54.460 --> 00:37:01.350
are producing innovations.

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:02.770
Now an innovation isn't a good thing.

00:37:02.770 --> 00:37:05.569
It just means it's a new thing.

00:37:05.569 --> 00:37:08.810
It might be amazing, it might be foolish.

00:37:08.810 --> 00:37:10.000
And the point on innovation is,

00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:15.600
we don't know until we try
the difference between folly and genius.

00:37:16.690 --> 00:37:20.152
Remember the stories of
Bruno Deleon in 1993,

00:37:20.152 --> 00:37:23.380
the end of industrial revolution in 1995.

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:24.870
And how we're so bad a predicting.

00:37:25.990 --> 00:37:28.510
With that lesson in mind.

00:37:28.510 --> 00:37:31.080
New ideas, new possibilities come along.

00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:32.550
They might be foolish.

00:37:32.550 --> 00:37:33.980
They might be genius.

00:37:35.000 --> 00:37:39.304
The year that Google was found, I don't
know what the distribution looks like.

00:37:39.304 --> 00:37:40.540
You know, that's optimistic.

00:37:40.540 --> 00:37:43.850
Maybe the distribution is that there's
a lot more foolishness out there.

00:37:43.850 --> 00:37:45.430
I don't know what
the distribution looks like.

00:37:46.880 --> 00:37:53.870
The year that Google was founded,
if you look in the archived press,

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:58.210
at the time everybody knew that
search was not a business.

00:37:59.270 --> 00:38:03.450
We had seen the failure of AltaVista,
and Excite, and

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:06.859
Lycos, and
you name your favorite dead search engine.

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:11.980
And so there was a lot of talk that,
well search is necessary,

00:38:11.980 --> 00:38:13.632
maybe it's a public utility.

00:38:13.632 --> 00:38:17.190
You know, we'll have PGNS, all right?

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:23.081
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Or PGNES, I don't know.

00:38:23.081 --> 00:38:27.664
Now, my marketing colleagues
tell me that search is clearly,

00:38:27.664 --> 00:38:32.275
the most valuable business model
in the history of humankind.

00:38:32.275 --> 00:38:36.100
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; We are so bad at predicting.

00:38:36.100 --> 00:38:39.500
And we are so
good at retrospectively rationalizing.

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.970
And so at the time,
we don't know what's here or what's here.

00:38:42.970 --> 00:38:46.400
And this is generally true,
by the way, this is generally true,

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.540
I mean it plagues all attempts
to increase innovation.

00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:54.300
It plagues all attempts
to increase innovation.

00:38:54.300 --> 00:38:58.620
And in companies you'll often see
a desire to have more innovation,

00:38:59.730 --> 00:39:01.640
but only to have smart innovation.

00:39:03.300 --> 00:39:05.436
Now, can we really get away with that?

00:39:05.436 --> 00:39:07.750
Yes, I don't think we can either.

00:39:09.160 --> 00:39:13.530
The city state of Singapore, one of
the great miracle economies of the world,

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:17.820
has a lot of different programs,
to try to improve it's economy.

00:39:17.820 --> 00:39:19.950
It's an amazing place.

00:39:19.950 --> 00:39:23.780
Some of you may have spent time in
Singapore, may be from Singapore.

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:27.260
But they actually tried to do this, and
their experiment is worth thinking about.

00:39:27.260 --> 00:39:29.850
They noticed that Singapore
was very efficient, but

00:39:29.850 --> 00:39:32.270
wasn't being innovative enough, and

00:39:32.270 --> 00:39:37.070
a few years ago had one of their campaigns
focus on increasing spontaneity.

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:44.170
So they wanted to bring people in from
the arts, and music, and create more of a,

00:39:44.170 --> 00:39:48.280
I guess a Greenwich Village
feel to Singapore.

00:39:52.120 --> 00:39:58.350
Now the trouble with being a street
musician in Singapore [LAUGH]

00:39:58.350 --> 00:40:00.600
This is, I'm remembering my youth.

00:40:00.600 --> 00:40:03.360
All my college and
graduate school was at Berkeley.

00:40:05.240 --> 00:40:08.092
Sometime you want to take a field
trip up but not all together.

00:40:08.092 --> 00:40:11.310
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] They'll know if they get

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:14.750
somebody, the Dean out in front,
you'll all go up.

00:40:14.750 --> 00:40:18.420
But go just independently up to Berkley
and check out the street musicians.

00:40:18.420 --> 00:40:20.880
It's not a pretty sight.

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:23.330
When the street musicians
started showing up in Singapore,

00:40:24.760 --> 00:40:26.270
they have a cleanliness campaign.

00:40:26.270 --> 00:40:30.710
They took them aside,
issued them a poncho and a playlist.

00:40:30.710 --> 00:40:32.545
They could play Peter,
Paul and Mary lyrics.

00:40:32.545 --> 00:40:34.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is true.

00:40:34.290 --> 00:40:35.580
This is actually what happened.

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:41.560
I think Puff the Magic Dragon had
a meaning that was not understood.

00:40:45.020 --> 00:40:47.100
We understood it in Berkeley,
I can tell you.

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:51.760
If you see what's going on,
they say we want innovation, but

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:53.070
we've been to Berkeley.

00:40:54.940 --> 00:40:57.910
So they say, what we're going to have
is we're going to have innovation, but

00:40:57.910 --> 00:40:59.500
we're not going to have any of this.

00:40:59.500 --> 00:41:00.770
We're not going to have any of this.

00:41:00.770 --> 00:41:01.835
That's much better.

00:41:01.835 --> 00:41:04.260
Uh-oh.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:05.400
My gosh.

00:41:05.400 --> 00:41:05.970
What happened?

00:41:07.280 --> 00:41:11.200
You see what happens is when we try
to control the innovation process and

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:15.610
have smart innovation and get rid of all
that, we think we're pushing up the mean.

00:41:17.090 --> 00:41:19.010
What we're doing is we're
pushing down the variance.

00:41:20.760 --> 00:41:24.600
When we think we're managing up the mean,
we're actually managing down the variance.

00:41:24.600 --> 00:41:26.870
Because by virtue of
getting rid of foolishness,

00:41:26.870 --> 00:41:30.850
what we're actually getting rid
of is non-consensus behavior.

00:41:30.850 --> 00:41:33.260
Because remember, we don't know
the difference between genius and

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:34.740
folly before the fact.

00:41:34.740 --> 00:41:37.655
All we know before the fact is
the difference between consensus and

00:41:37.655 --> 00:41:39.560
non-consensus behavior.

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:40.950
Bob Dylan can't sing.

00:41:45.130 --> 00:41:46.720
I think he just won the Nobel Prize.

00:41:49.510 --> 00:41:53.600
We'll cut him some slack on his ability
to hit every note in the scale.

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:56.770
I'm sure we would have cleaned him out.

00:41:58.720 --> 00:42:00.670
You see what I'm getting at here?

00:42:00.670 --> 00:42:04.860
There's even a historian of science who
has looked at all of the Albert Einstein's

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:08.740
work and
found actually just arithmetic errors.

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:14.460
I tell you what,
if you can come up with a general theory,

00:42:16.270 --> 00:42:18.285
we'll cut you some slack
on your arithmetic.

00:42:18.285 --> 00:42:22.662
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And that's the idea, that in the search

00:42:22.662 --> 00:42:27.766
for genius, if you want genius, look for
systems that create foolishness.

00:42:30.203 --> 00:42:34.277
If you find a system that's
creating foolishness,

00:42:34.277 --> 00:42:37.550
there might be a chance of genius.

00:42:37.550 --> 00:42:40.170
If you're finding a system that
never does anything foolish,

00:42:40.170 --> 00:42:41.480
there's no way you're getting genius.

00:42:43.540 --> 00:42:47.480
So that's the first property of these
systems, if you want you organization

00:42:47.480 --> 00:42:50.680
to discover greatness,
it has to do something different.

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:57.800
Not only different it has to do something
different that is non-consensus.

00:42:57.800 --> 00:43:01.320
Start thinking about
business plan competitions.

00:43:01.320 --> 00:43:02.340
The consensus pick?

00:43:05.060 --> 00:43:05.790
Not genius.

00:43:07.060 --> 00:43:11.880
In fact, what I want to see in a business
plan competition is the plan that

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:17.060
generates the biggest argument,
the future Irwin Jacobs.

00:43:18.665 --> 00:43:19.890
You see where I'm going with that.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:21.630
You've got to manage for variance.

00:43:21.630 --> 00:43:24.590
Now managing for variance is something
you can do in some companies,

00:43:24.590 --> 00:43:25.680
you can't do for others.

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:30.690
With being an ancient professor,

00:43:30.690 --> 00:43:34.520
you get more phone calls to go
talk to people and companies.

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:40.150
I get calls all the time from people who
should really be behaving predictably and

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:42.170
they say,
we want some of this innovation stuff.

00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:45.240
I said, no, you really don't.

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.280
You do not want me to
come talk to your people.

00:43:49.330 --> 00:43:52.230
I was called recently by an organization
on the other side of the world that runs

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:53.455
nuclear power plants.

00:43:53.455 --> 00:43:57.000
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you don't want me near them.

00:43:57.000 --> 00:44:02.850
[LAUGH]

00:44:02.850 --> 00:44:05.700
I want you to do it so predictably.

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:09.520
I want you to be Mr. Consensus.

00:44:09.520 --> 00:44:13.220
The person who flies my airplane,
I want them to take off and

00:44:13.220 --> 00:44:15.660
land the exact same way every time.

00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:19.429
There's plenty of context where I don't
want experimentation and foolishness.

00:44:20.460 --> 00:44:22.570
And I'm not going to kid myself and

00:44:22.570 --> 00:44:24.550
say that I'm only going to
have smart innovation.

00:44:24.550 --> 00:44:27.790
I'm going to say that a system has
to create variance, in order to.

00:44:27.790 --> 00:44:30.617
So that's the biggest job.

00:44:30.617 --> 00:44:33.315
Job one, variability.

00:44:36.596 --> 00:44:41.410
If your system creates variation, you
have a chance of discovering greatness.

00:44:41.410 --> 00:44:44.340
The second point is you have
to select from that variation.

00:44:45.520 --> 00:44:49.410
And that's what all those little
stories were about Airbnb, and SAMx,

00:44:49.410 --> 00:44:52.860
and Apple and
you name your favorite organization,

00:44:52.860 --> 00:44:56.840
Trader Joe, your favorite organization
that discovered its greatness.

00:44:57.910 --> 00:45:03.180
Leadership saw the possibility and

00:45:03.180 --> 00:45:06.610
was able to select that
new possibility and

00:45:06.610 --> 00:45:11.240
turn it into a strategy and
it's interesting.

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:12.950
If Apple had stayed with its strategy,

00:45:12.950 --> 00:45:16.360
it would have been a computer company,
it probably would be history.

00:45:16.360 --> 00:45:19.220
Certainly, wouldn't be the most
valuable company on Earth.

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:22.520
Airbnb same thing, would have been worth
a few million if they would have nailed it

00:45:22.520 --> 00:45:26.700
on event lodging organizing and so forth.

00:45:27.730 --> 00:45:31.920
Often what we discover in
the process of creating variation

00:45:31.920 --> 00:45:36.780
has much more potential than whatever
it was we were planning to begin with.

00:45:36.780 --> 00:45:40.760
And that process of selection
is typically iterative, or

00:45:40.760 --> 00:45:45.590
we start with our strategy,
try to see how it works in

00:45:45.590 --> 00:45:50.450
the market and then update our
strategy on this iterative way.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:57.990
If we see strategy less as a planning
process and more as a discovery process,

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:03.510
now we have a chance of turning that
variability coming from our people,

00:46:03.510 --> 00:46:08.350
those new ideas into reality.

00:46:08.350 --> 00:46:13.044
The third part is retention or

00:46:13.044 --> 00:46:17.580
growth, where we retain the ideas
that work best and we grow them.

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:24.540
And that's a tough part because if this
is over time and that's performance,

00:46:24.540 --> 00:46:27.799
everybody knows when you change strategy,
what happens to your performance?

00:46:29.110 --> 00:46:29.610
Goes down.

00:46:32.080 --> 00:46:36.320
It takes a while to get a great
strategy's performance up.

00:46:36.320 --> 00:46:40.410
The performances going to go up,
only after we survive the J curve.

00:46:42.030 --> 00:46:45.110
How are we going to get through that
decline in performance as we're going in

00:46:45.110 --> 00:46:45.880
a new direction?

00:46:47.220 --> 00:46:52.320
We have to be able to get our people to
understand the logic of that new strategy

00:46:53.880 --> 00:46:55.700
and that's leadership looking forward.

00:46:57.320 --> 00:46:59.230
We connect the dots looking backwards.

00:47:00.350 --> 00:47:04.190
That's the discovery process right here.

00:47:06.850 --> 00:47:10.950
We connect the dots looking
backwards as Steve Job said in his,

00:47:10.950 --> 00:47:13.860
I guess it was 2008,
his commencement address here.

00:47:13.860 --> 00:47:15.930
He said, you can only connect
the dots looking backwards.

00:47:17.550 --> 00:47:20.620
Looking forward, you have to believe
that the dots are going to connect.

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:26.981
Leadership has to give its people the
logic that has them believe that that's

00:47:26.981 --> 00:47:27.720
possible.

00:47:29.894 --> 00:47:32.240
You see where great leaders come from.

00:47:32.240 --> 00:47:36.100
Great leaders are people who understand
that it is not their job to know

00:47:36.100 --> 00:47:36.720
the future.

00:47:37.730 --> 00:47:41.070
It is their job to create a system
that discovers that future.

00:47:42.520 --> 00:47:46.530
In leading that system,
leaders perform two functions.

00:47:48.920 --> 00:47:51.280
Looking back,
they retrospectively rationalize.

00:47:51.280 --> 00:47:54.130
They make sense out of why your
people are doing what they're doing.

00:47:54.130 --> 00:47:59.020
Looking forward,
They turn that into a logic, a strategy,

00:47:59.020 --> 00:48:03.750
that helps their people survive the tough
times, when they try to change the world.

00:48:03.750 --> 00:48:08.030
because changing the world is difficult,
and it takes time, but

00:48:08.030 --> 00:48:11.110
when you persist,
you create something great.

00:48:12.890 --> 00:48:15.434
Where else have you heard variation,
selection and retention?

00:48:15.434 --> 00:48:19.731
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; It's the Theory of Evolution.

00:48:19.731 --> 00:48:26.980
Economies evolve just like the biosphere.

00:48:26.980 --> 00:48:31.530
The difference is that,
in this process, we play a role.

00:48:32.820 --> 00:48:35.280
It isn't blind variation.

00:48:35.280 --> 00:48:39.450
It's variation among humans,
guided by leaders,

00:48:39.450 --> 00:48:44.620
in some cases who make non
consensus behavior seem criminal.

00:48:44.620 --> 00:48:46.130
And in other cases, who encourage that.

00:48:47.680 --> 00:48:49.650
It's selection, but

00:48:49.650 --> 00:48:54.120
selection mediated by leaders,
who create systems that try and learn.

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:57.020
Some organizations that learning,

00:48:57.020 --> 00:49:01.750
because it's fraught with failure,
damns people's careers.

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:04.233
In other organizations,
that learning is seen as constructive.

00:49:06.767 --> 00:49:11.755
And it's retention in the form of growth,
which in some organizations is only safe

00:49:11.755 --> 00:49:15.120
if you can turn over a result
in the next week or quarter.

00:49:17.650 --> 00:49:19.070
And don't blame the public markets.

00:49:22.600 --> 00:49:23.910
Always look to leadership.

00:49:26.790 --> 00:49:30.820
Because the public markets,
like any capital market,

00:49:30.820 --> 00:49:33.990
have to be persuaded,
that that logic makes sense.

00:49:35.370 --> 00:49:37.970
To believe long enough to get you there.

00:49:37.970 --> 00:49:41.690
So whenever you see yourself pointing at
someone else, turn that finger back, and

00:49:41.690 --> 00:49:45.180
ask yourself as a leader,
what role do you play in this process?

00:49:45.180 --> 00:49:48.680
Are you increasing your organization's
chances of becoming great?

00:49:49.710 --> 00:49:50.970
Because it isn't random luck.

00:49:53.860 --> 00:49:55.150
It's all about great leadership.

00:49:56.760 --> 00:49:58.781
So with that we have five minutes.

00:49:58.781 --> 00:49:59.680
Any questions?

00:49:59.680 --> 00:50:05.228
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Okay,

00:50:05.228 --> 00:50:08.693
the question was,
why was I blocked from China.

00:50:08.693 --> 00:50:12.630
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not blocked from China.

00:50:12.630 --> 00:50:16.120
So I go to China freely.

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:18.673
Well, not freely, I got to get a visa and
do the whole thing.

00:50:18.673 --> 00:50:23.116
But it's just, you can't,
I'm not really sure why, I don't think I

00:50:23.116 --> 00:50:27.760
said anything too outlandish, but
you can't reach my blog from China.

00:50:27.760 --> 00:50:32.490
So yeah, no I wish there was a great
really interesting story where I had to,

00:50:32.490 --> 00:50:34.930
like with Tom Cruise getting in a jet,
and escaping.

00:50:34.930 --> 00:50:38.133
But it was just really,
[LAUGH] they just don't like my ideas.

00:50:38.133 --> 00:50:41.940
&gt;&gt; Given your lecture, can you give us
some good questions to ask at the next

00:50:41.940 --> 00:50:45.715
lecture on the 2016 Presidential Election?

00:50:45.715 --> 00:50:50.572
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:50:50.572 --> 00:50:58.344
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:58.344 --> 00:51:01.911
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Yeah.

00:51:01.911 --> 00:51:02.847
It's amazing.

00:51:02.847 --> 00:51:05.516
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]

00:51:05.516 --> 00:51:08.112
&gt;&gt; No, I'm not going to say anything, so.

00:51:08.112 --> 00:51:08.624
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:51:08.624 --> 00:51:11.780
&gt;&gt; We're just going to let somebody ask

00:51:11.780 --> 00:51:12.612
another question.

00:51:12.612 --> 00:51:17.590
[LAUGH] So because your going to have
a whole talk on that with people who,

00:51:17.590 --> 00:51:21.170
okay looks like we had a question coming
up here, and then I'll come to you.

00:51:21.170 --> 00:51:22.280
Go ahead.

00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:24.019
&gt;&gt; Question about measurement
in this whole process.

00:51:24.019 --> 00:51:26.337
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; A lot of these company examples,

00:51:26.337 --> 00:51:30.622
you said they were measuring things,
and they realized then to shift past.

00:51:30.622 --> 00:51:32.603
&gt;&gt; Yes.
&gt;&gt; But measurement always has a bias,

00:51:32.603 --> 00:51:34.222
right, it focuses you on something.

00:51:34.222 --> 00:51:36.198
So if you could just talk about
how that relates to all this.

00:51:36.198 --> 00:51:38.270
&gt;&gt; Sure, sure, that's a great question.

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:41.350
So folks who are really familiar
with this part of the process,

00:51:41.350 --> 00:51:47.800
understand that it's become a science or
pseudo-science these days,

00:51:47.800 --> 00:51:52.660
where you look at
the feedback from testing.

00:51:52.660 --> 00:51:54.700
You try to fail fast and cheap.

00:51:54.700 --> 00:51:58.680
You look at the feedback from testing, and
then update on the basis of that feedback.

00:51:59.900 --> 00:52:05.010
And those of you who study the engineering
of searching for optimized, sort of local

00:52:05.010 --> 00:52:09.660
search, and in iterative optimization,
if those terms are appealing to you.

00:52:09.660 --> 00:52:14.990
You know that this kind of measurement
suffers from a variety of problems that

00:52:14.990 --> 00:52:16.670
often are talked about as bias.

00:52:19.500 --> 00:52:23.170
So probably the biggest problem is
that it keeps you extremely focused

00:52:23.170 --> 00:52:26.460
on short-term results.

00:52:26.460 --> 00:52:29.837
So if you look here, if we're doing
AB testing of our new idea here,

00:52:29.837 --> 00:52:30.920
we're going to pivot.

00:52:32.330 --> 00:52:33.900
People love pivoting these days,

00:52:35.030 --> 00:52:38.050
you gotta just go have a glass of
wine on University Avenue, and

00:52:38.050 --> 00:52:41.845
listen to the young people talk, and
they all say the word pivot all the time.

00:52:41.845 --> 00:52:43.043
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And so,

00:52:43.043 --> 00:52:46.429
the recipe is that you try something,
it doesn't work, you pivot, you're rich.

00:52:46.429 --> 00:52:47.397
That's kind of the recipe.

00:52:47.397 --> 00:52:51.318
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:52:51.318 --> 00:52:58.277
the problem is that great firms,
if they pivot here, they never get there.

00:52:58.277 --> 00:53:03.461
And so, you do want to pivot,
if the very logic that

00:53:03.461 --> 00:53:09.350
you think is true here,
is failing it's test.

00:53:09.350 --> 00:53:12.660
But, if all you're doing is just looking
at a result, without thinking through

00:53:12.660 --> 00:53:17.260
the logic of the business,
you want to think again before you pivot.

00:53:17.260 --> 00:53:18.990
A second problem you can run into,

00:53:18.990 --> 00:53:23.770
is that even if the results are speaking
to the direction of your firm,

00:53:23.770 --> 00:53:26.350
you can get false positives, and
you can get false negatives.

00:53:27.650 --> 00:53:31.431
Right, so a false positive,
is where you think you did a good job, but

00:53:31.431 --> 00:53:35.804
it actually it's the thing you did is a
bad thing, you just got a false positive.

00:53:35.804 --> 00:53:40.038
And a false negative is where,
what you are doing is a good idea, but

00:53:40.038 --> 00:53:44.970
your results are saying it was a bad idea,
that's a false negative.

00:53:44.970 --> 00:53:47.195
Now what's better,
a false negative or a false positive?

00:53:47.195 --> 00:53:49.373
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:53:49.373 --> 00:53:52.388
everybody loves false negatives better,
because that means you're right.

00:53:52.388 --> 00:53:54.398
But here is the problem.

00:53:54.398 --> 00:53:57.480
False positives are self correcting.

00:53:58.700 --> 00:53:59.620
False negatives are not.

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:02.535
because if I get a false negative,
I'll pivot.

00:54:04.290 --> 00:54:05.830
And I won't realize I was right.

00:54:07.610 --> 00:54:10.410
A false positive,
I'm going to do it again, and

00:54:10.410 --> 00:54:14.650
then I'm going to find out hold on
[LAUGH], turns out that was not correct.

00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:17.690
So you actually get a larger sample,
the great professor March,

00:54:17.690 --> 00:54:23.010
some of you had when you were here, has an
article on the issue the young man raised.

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:25.290
People don't read the articles so
much, they should.

00:54:25.290 --> 00:54:26.490
They definitely read the title.

00:54:27.720 --> 00:54:33.551
Learning from samples of one or fewer.

00:54:33.551 --> 00:54:35.077
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Don't you wish you had written

00:54:35.077 --> 00:54:36.470
the paper with that title.

00:54:36.470 --> 00:54:39.000
Even if, you know what I mean?

00:54:39.000 --> 00:54:43.790
And, that's exactly what happens with
a false negative, is you say, my gosh,

00:54:43.790 --> 00:54:46.271
I've done that and boy you should
see what happened, I'm pivoting.

00:54:47.610 --> 00:54:51.842
And so you want to run the test again,
there's a sense in which we have to try to

00:54:51.842 --> 00:54:54.905
get to a sample size that
resembles something decent.

00:54:54.905 --> 00:55:00.499
Of course big data companies have massive
amounts of data, and can do that but,

00:55:00.499 --> 00:55:06.360
when it comes to strategy, we'll often run
the test just once, and then shy away.

00:55:06.360 --> 00:55:09.400
And so we'll come to this gentleman, and
then we'll come to this gentleman, and

00:55:09.400 --> 00:55:10.960
I think that then,
we'll probably have to close.

00:55:10.960 --> 00:55:12.290
So, yeah, go ahead.

00:55:12.290 --> 00:55:17.170
&gt;&gt; Would you explain how,
or even if your theory

00:55:17.170 --> 00:55:22.110
would apply to either, the not for profit
sector, or more importantly, government?

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:27.830
&gt;&gt; Sure, yes,
absolutely it applies to both.

00:55:27.830 --> 00:55:31.640
This way of looking at
the world does apply to both.

00:55:31.640 --> 00:55:39.960
And but what changes, is the criteria for
deciding on success and failure.

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:44.260
That obviously in the examples I've been
giving, I talked a lot about a market.

00:55:44.260 --> 00:55:47.400
And I've talked about success and
failure in that market.

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:50.600
And this performance, presumably,
is market performance.

00:55:50.600 --> 00:55:56.280
And we talked about companies being
profitable and growing, and so forth.

00:55:56.280 --> 00:56:01.980
In the not for profit sector,
experimentation and performance

00:56:01.980 --> 00:56:06.480
also have, they do have to have or
there has to be some kind of returns.

00:56:06.480 --> 00:56:11.450
But those returns are a function of
the legitimacy of the organization.

00:56:12.480 --> 00:56:16.240
So, legitimacy is the currency
of the not for profit world.

00:56:16.240 --> 00:56:19.900
And if I consider the activities
of a particular organization to be

00:56:19.900 --> 00:56:24.770
especially legitimate and
praiseworthy then funding sources and

00:56:24.770 --> 00:56:27.780
people who give grants and
other kinds of support will fall in line.

00:56:27.780 --> 00:56:29.880
And that's also true in government.

00:56:29.880 --> 00:56:34.330
Legitimacy is the currency in
the public sector generally.

00:56:34.330 --> 00:56:38.425
And so, if you think about it,
legitimacy creates a real problem here,

00:56:38.425 --> 00:56:42.200
because often, the experiments
that go awry look illegitimate.

00:56:44.751 --> 00:56:48.315
And, we often see risk
averseness in the public sector,

00:56:48.315 --> 00:56:52.260
precisely because we're worried
about being illegitimate.

00:56:53.460 --> 00:56:58.120
And so, public sector leaders also
have to think about creating context

00:56:58.120 --> 00:57:00.440
where it's safe to deviate.

00:57:01.740 --> 00:57:06.100
Even though it might seem like
that would threaten legitimacy.

00:57:06.100 --> 00:57:11.690
And similarly with selection, if we go
through a process of fail, fast and cheap.

00:57:11.690 --> 00:57:16.370
And we try different things in the public
sector or in not for profit organizations

00:57:16.370 --> 00:57:20.510
and we fail, grant-giving organizations,
other kinds of donors and

00:57:20.510 --> 00:57:23.380
funders are going to look and
say, well what's the story here?

00:57:23.380 --> 00:57:24.980
We're seeing a high failure rate.

00:57:24.980 --> 00:57:27.260
We would like to see a success rate.

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:30.940
And where you're hurting there is,
once again, in your legitimacy.

00:57:30.940 --> 00:57:34.740
And the inability to grow comes down to
the fact that when you're busy changing

00:57:34.740 --> 00:57:38.900
the world you can have an legitimacy
crisis during this period as well, but

00:57:38.900 --> 00:57:40.520
if you can take the same ideas, and

00:57:40.520 --> 00:57:43.520
those of you who work in
not-for-profit and public sector

00:57:43.520 --> 00:57:48.260
simply translate them into the performance
metrics that are appropriate for you.

00:57:48.260 --> 00:57:51.230
Because evolution happens in the not for
profit section too.

00:57:51.230 --> 00:57:54.670
Evolution and
discovery is the way of our world.

00:57:55.940 --> 00:57:56.620
Sir.

00:57:56.620 --> 00:57:57.390
&gt;&gt; Bill Levi, MBA 71.

00:57:57.390 --> 00:57:59.560
Good job.
You're great, thank you.

00:57:59.560 --> 00:58:01.040
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:04.769
&gt;&gt; I propose to all of us that
the greatest business opportunity of our

00:58:04.769 --> 00:58:08.899
time is transforming the world's
largest industry from 85% fossil to

00:58:08.899 --> 00:58:13.560
100% renewables as quickly as we
prudently and profitably can do that.

00:58:13.560 --> 00:58:20.250
Where's the great leadership in the energy
industry, and why are they so slow?

00:58:20.250 --> 00:58:21.520
Picking up, time's running out.

00:58:22.580 --> 00:58:28.730
&gt;&gt; Well, I applaud that question, and I'm
thrilled to have it be our last question,

00:58:28.730 --> 00:58:33.050
because I think it's probably the most
important question we face today.

00:58:35.900 --> 00:58:38.940
And I think we need
leadership on many fronts.

00:58:40.020 --> 00:58:45.430
A large number of scholars
at the Woods Institute

00:58:45.430 --> 00:58:49.430
here are working on leadership
in various sectors.

00:58:49.430 --> 00:58:53.360
And our scientists have obviously
led the way, Chris Field and others,

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:57.420
the author if the IPCC reports
over the years on climate change.

00:58:57.420 --> 00:59:02.380
But we also have a big push in
Earth sciences and among a number

00:59:02.380 --> 00:59:07.340
of the faculty in the business school and
in engineering trying to show the way, but

00:59:07.340 --> 00:59:11.830
it is going to require a lot of effort,
a lot of collective effort.

00:59:11.830 --> 00:59:14.400
I think we're to the point
we're in this period.

00:59:15.420 --> 00:59:23.150
and the difficulty is many of the changes
that we need to make to move to an economy

00:59:23.150 --> 00:59:28.150
that allows for
sustainability worldwide are going to,

00:59:28.150 --> 00:59:31.380
they're going to be difficult to implement
in the sense that during this period

00:59:31.380 --> 00:59:34.950
they're going to create a lot of problems
and difficulties in performance.

00:59:34.950 --> 00:59:39.480
It takes a great deal of courage
to execute on ideas like that, and

00:59:39.480 --> 00:59:45.230
while we're making progress, we really
are not making progress fast enough.

00:59:45.230 --> 00:59:51.440
And for some years, I ran here at
the school an executive program

00:59:51.440 --> 00:59:55.786
on this topic, and
we brought in business leaders,

00:59:55.786 --> 01:00:00.710
government leaders and leaders from
social movement organizations and

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:03.470
not for profits,
all three in equal numbers.

01:00:05.120 --> 01:00:07.410
And the first day it was
always the scientists.

01:00:09.200 --> 01:00:14.130
And then the other days of program were
the economists and sociologists and

01:00:14.130 --> 01:00:17.510
other people on the business school
talking about what we can do based on.

01:00:17.510 --> 01:00:18.040
But I can tell you,

01:00:18.040 --> 01:00:22.090
by the end of that first day It
was a pretty sobering message.

01:00:22.090 --> 01:00:23.820
And that message remains very sobering.

01:00:24.960 --> 01:00:27.720
And you have to remember that the truth
is the truth whether you want

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:28.410
to hear it or not.

01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:33.320
And the important part of first changing
the world is first to get your head around

01:00:33.320 --> 01:00:34.220
what's true.

01:00:34.220 --> 01:00:39.920
And what's true is that we do face
something that is monumental in

01:00:39.920 --> 01:00:43.700
terms of a problem when it comes to moving
away from the fossil fuel economy and

01:00:43.700 --> 01:00:45.390
the need to deal with climate change.

01:00:46.490 --> 01:00:51.110
Now, translating that into action depends
on where you work and as a leader where

01:00:51.110 --> 01:00:55.140
you're going to do this, in the public
sector there's some people trying to show

01:00:55.140 --> 01:00:59.280
leadership in the private sector many of
our alums are trying to show leadership.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:02.570
It sounds like this gentleman
is also showing leadership

01:01:02.570 --> 01:01:04.190
in his part of the world.

01:01:04.190 --> 01:01:07.170
I think faculty and researchers
need to show leadership as well.

01:01:08.280 --> 01:01:12.920
When you have a movement across all of
those fronts of people showing leadership

01:01:12.920 --> 01:01:18.150
we have an amazing ability as
a species to solve problems,

01:01:18.150 --> 01:01:22.020
and come up with greatness in ways
that we never would have expected.

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:25.520
So, I think this is a domain
where my message that,

01:01:25.520 --> 01:01:29.270
we're not here to predict the future,
we're here to create the systems that

01:01:29.270 --> 01:01:33.500
discover that future is a message
that needs to be heard.

01:01:33.500 --> 01:01:35.055
Thank you very much, and
thank you all for coming back.

01:01:35.055 --> 01:01:39.382
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

01:01:39.382 --> 01:01:43.540
[MUSIC]

